levels below Dlvl 14 were like Gehennom is to you.  

  In my current game, I ended up going as deep as Dlvl 20 before 
fully developing my Wizard.  So there 'll be a little less 
boredom.


  OTOH, after a seemingly infinite number of player deaths, I
find it rather pleasant to cruise through the dungeon with few
worries.  YMOV.

  It's the nature of the game that once you've reached your
personal limit on overpreparation, you want to just finish the 
game.

  Play a little faster so that items you may get in Gehennom can
still be useful to you, and Gehennom will be a more interesting,
although not always in a good way. ;^)

  The only way I can see to force each player to find each new 
level interesting is to strictly tie character development to
current Dungeon level.  

  I.e. Character stats and equipment will have a hard limit on 
how well they can be developed, based on current dungeon level.
The easy way would be that your character stats could only be
improved up to (Dlvl/60)%.  If you start out with 50% of max 
strength for your character, you couldn't hope to improve it 
until Dlvl 31.

  I don't think it's a good idea.  But I don't see any other
way to avoid the boredom that results from over preparation.


  If you just lower the current hard limits on character 
development, players will still get to that limit as soon as 
possible, making the rest of the dungeon simply a trial to 
complete.


  So it's not so much that Gehennom is inherently uninteresting, 
it's that you're over prepared by the time you get to it.

  Under preparation can make the rest of the Dungeons a lot more 
interesting, and not in a good way.  So you get to choose between
boredom and terror, or trying to walk the fine line in between.


  If you over prepare properly ;^), everything below Dlvl 14 can 
be uninteresting.  How's that for something to look forward to?




